0 < 1 # The fleet contract (v4, 2026-09-29 — the evidence repository owns the record). Every builder reads this first.
1 < 1 
2 < 1 Design record: `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-03-fleet-on-the-grain.md`, whose `## Counsel 2` section
3 < 1 (Sol + Opus on the papercuts of runs 65–69) is the authority for the sandbox internals below, and
4 < 1 issues #597/#598 for the shape of a launch. Where v2 of this file (git history) and this text
5 < 1 disagree, this text wins; v4 (#1395, the operator's picks and ruling of 2026-09-29) moved the plan
6 < 1 and the record off the target into the operator's evidence repository. This contract is the launcher's and the fleet VM's; the engine itself is
7 < 1 the Flock (`factory/flock/`), the one engine since the factory's retirement (map #1292 rule 8), and
8 < 1 its own literals are not restated here. The wave engine this file described in detail
9 < 1 until 2026-09-21 (cut two) is gone; `fleet/RUNBOOK.md` names the rollback.
10 < 1 
11 < 1 ## The shape in one paragraph
12 < 1 A run is a number N per target, and its plan and record live in the operator's EVIDENCE REPOSITORY,
13 < 1 never on the target. That repository is the operator's own setting — the key `"evidence"` (value
14 < 1 `<owner>/<repo>`; the operator's is `popmechanic/fleet-evidence`, private) in
15 < 1 `~/.ultrapowers/fleet.json`, overridden per launch by `--evidence-repo <owner>/<repo>` — and is never
16 < 1 derived from the target; a launch with neither is refused, naming the key and `node fleet/doctor.mjs`.
17 < 1 Because the record always goes to the operator's repository, a foreign target (someone else's
18 < 1 repository) and another user's fleet both work: a `facebook/react` run lands in
19 < 1 `runs/facebook-react/<N>/` of the operator's repository. A run's folder is
21 > 1 `runs/<owner>-<repo>/<N>/` (the target's owner and repo): the plan files `plan.md` and
20 < 2 `runs/<owner>-<repo>/<N>/` (the target's owner and repo): the plan files `plan.md`, `kata.json` and
21 < 1 `gate-verdicts.json`, and every record file — `status.json`, `plan.json` (the run's one parse, #1486), `events.jsonl`, `engine.log`,
22 < 1 `fleet-boot.log`, `fleet-setup.log`, `summary.json`, the engine files and the publish files. The launcher validates its arguments, reads
23 < 1 the account pool from `billing plan --json`, computes N from the evidence repository's
24 < 1 `live/<owner>-<repo>/run-*` branches and `<owner>-<repo>/run-*` tags, refreshes the Claude bearer, and
25 < 1 pushes the plan as ONE parentless commit in the evidence repository — its tree that folder with the
26 < 1 plan files, its message naming the target and the base sha — to `live/<owner>-<repo>/run-<N>`. Then it
27 < 1 issues ONE lobby verb — `new` — which creates a fresh VM and runs the generated setup script on it.
28 < 1 The setup script writes the evidence repository to `$HOME/fleet-evidence-repo`, installs the
29 < 1 toolchain, an immutable bootstrap and the run's unit, then starts `fleet-run@<N>.service`. The
30 < 1 bootstrap reads the assignment from the VM comment once, clones the engine at `engine=` into a
31 < 1 content-addressed directory, and execs that checkout's `factory/boot.sh`. The boot script clones the
32 < 1 target at `base=`, fetches only the live branch of the evidence repository, shallow, runs the engine
34 > 1 as a transient user service with a memory cap, commits the record to the live branch every 10 s and
33 < 2 as a transient user service with a memory cap, commits the record to the live branch every 60 s and
34 < 1 at each transition — no status page; git is the record — and, only when there is something to publish
35 < 1 and the default branch moved underneath it, catches the run up to the target's tip inline
36 < 1 (`factory/flock/catchup.mjs`) before the PR, then pushes `ultra/integration-run-N` to the target and
37 < 1 opens the PR over GitHub's REST API through the edge. At the end of every run (done, parked and
38 < 1 failed) it cuts one tag `<owner>-<repo>/run-<N>` on the live branch's last commit, verifies it with
39 < 1 `git ls-remote --tags`, and deletes the live branch. A `done` run whose PR merged, not held, or that opened no PR (a stories-v1 run with nothing to build), and
40 < 1 whose tag verified then asks `https://reaper.int.exe.xyz/reap` once with `{run, target}`, and the hub
42 > 1 removes the calling VM only when the run's `status.json` at that tag, read from the evidence
44 > 1 repository, says `state` `done` (#1470); the janitor still reaps what
43 < 2 repository, says `state` `done` (#1470; a run issue closed `done` on the hub's kata is the rollback,
44 < 2 `REAPER_RECORD=kata` in the hub's `/etc/reaper/reaper.env`); the janitor still reaps what
41 < 2 removes the calling VM only when the run issue is closed `done` (#1470); the janitor still reaps what
42 < 1 never asks. The PR is the human gate: the target's
43 < 1 integration and the evidence repository's ride the VM for the run's whole life, and there is no
44 < 1 grant step. There is no image to keep fresh, no orchestrator, no control VM, and no token on any VM.
45 < 1 The product repository receives only `ultra/integration-run-<N>`; what a run leaves is one tag in the
46 < 1 evidence repository.
47 < 1 
48 < 1 ## Literals
49 < 1 - **Run id:** `N` = 1 + max N over the evidence repository's `live/<owner>-<repo>/run-<N>` branches
50 < 1   and its `<owner>-<repo>/run-<N>` tags (`<owner>-<repo>` the target's) — the branches are transient
51 < 1   and the tags are the record, so a run number is read from both shapes and never from one.
52 < 1   A refused plan push re-reads
53 < 1   the highest run and retries with the next N, up to three pushes in all, so the push and not the
54 < 1   read is what reserves N. `RUN_ID=run-N`.
55 < 1 - **VM name:** `fleet-r<N>-<yymmddHHMM>-<4 hex>` (e.g. `fleet-r70-2609032215-a1b2`). exe.dev does not reserve
56 < 1   deleted names, but a name is still one incarnation, never derived from N alone: the run number
57 < 1   is the identity. Lookup by pattern:
58 < 1   `ssh exe.dev "ls 'fleet-r<N>-*' --json"`; the whole fleet: `ls 'fleet-r*' --json`. Read `.vms[]` ONLY
59 < 1   (`.shared_vms` are other people's). Contractual row fields: `vm_name`, `ssh_dest`, `ssh_host`, `status`.
60 < 1   `comment`, `tags`, `created_at` are undocumented: read them as optional, never crash on their absence,
61 < 1   never decide from `created_at`. Use `ssh_dest` for ssh/scp, never `<vm_name>.exe.xyz`.
62 < 1 - **The two branches** — where a run works, not what it leaves; each one is deleted when the thing it
63 < 1   carried has landed (nothing else the fleet writes lives anywhere else):
64 < 1   - `live/<owner>-<repo>/run-<N>` in the evidence repository — the plan commit, then the record on
65 < 1     top of it. The plan commit is parentless; its tree is `runs/<owner>-<repo>/<N>/` holding
67 > 1     `plan.md` [+ `gate-verdicts.json`], its message naming the target and
66 < 2     `plan.md` [+ `gate-verdicts.json`] + `kata.json` (the run's record on the hub —
67 < 2     `{"url":"https://kata.int.exe.xyz","project":{id,uid,name},"run":{uid,revision},"tasks":{"<id>":{uid,short_id,revision}}}`,
68 < 2     keys in that order, each `revision` the one the launcher's post-link `getIssue` of that issue
69 < 2     answered and each task's `short_id` the one its `createIssue` answered — that is what a worker's
70 < 2     `KATA_REF=<project>#<short_id>` is built from, so a create answer without one is a refusal and no
71 < 2     record; `JSON.stringify(…, null, 2)` plus a trailing newline), its message naming the target and
72 < 1     the base sha. Written by the launcher, before any VM exists.
73 < 1     On top of it the sandbox commits the run's record into the same folder: `status.json`,
74 < 1     `events.jsonl` and `engine.log`, committed at every state transition and, while the engine
75 < 1     runs, on the first tick that finds `events.jsonl` changed since the last commit, at most
77 > 1     `FLEET_COMMIT_SECONDS` seconds apart (default 10) — so the branch is never far behind the
76 < 2     `FLEET_COMMIT_SECONDS` seconds apart (default 60) — so the branch is never far behind the
77 < 1     live run, and a tick that saw no change commits nothing however long it has been.
78 < 1     **This bullet described the wave engine's own record here in detail through 2026-09-21** —
79 < 1     `report.json`'s per-run schema, the `driver:wave-adopted`/`driver:wave-blocked`/
80 < 1     `driver:finding` event kinds, the seven kinds of receipt, the `FACTS:` block, `frontier/`,
81 < 1     `state-exams/`, `residuals.jsonl`, `kata.jsonl`'s per-event hub export and the fold-again
82 < 1     receipts directory — and it left with that engine at cut two; what the current engine writes
83 < 1     to this branch beyond the three files above is not yet described in this contract.
84 < 1     **A Flock run adds eight files beside them** (with `summary.json` and the
85 < 1     publish files), so its record says what went wrong and not only that it did:
86 < 1     - `board.json` — the board as it stood at the end: every task with its state and owner, and
87 < 1       the beliefs.
88 < 1     - `weave-ops.digest.jsonl` — every merge the weave made, one operation per line, with each
89 < 1       `content` replaced by `content_sha1` and `content_bytes`, so file texts are fingerprints.
90 < 1     - `snapshots.jsonl` — one row per tested snapshot: its hash, time, the paths changed or
91 < 1       deleted against base (each changed text with its sha1 and bytes), `from` (the previous
92 < 1       row's `snap`, or `base` for the first row) and `patch`, a unified diff against `from` that
93 < 1       `git apply` accepts; applying the rows' patches in order onto base rebuilds each tested
94 < 1       snapshot, and no file text is copied whole. The patches total at most 524288 bytes; a row
95 < 1       whose patch did not fit carries `patch: null` and `truncated: true`.
96 < 1     - `red-checks.json` — every check red at the last tested snapshot: its task, clause, command,
97 < 1       exit, output tail and the checker's own result when there is one, at most 65536 bytes.
98 < 1     - `past.json` — the previous run's items the builders were briefed with: `{run, items}`, at
99 < 1       most 10 items of at most 300 characters each. Written only when the previous run on the
100 < 1       target did not end `done`. The previous run is the one with the highest
101 < 1       `<owner>-<repo>/run-<M>` tag below this run's number in the evidence repository; the boot
102 < 1       extracts its folder into a directory the engine reads (`--past-dir`), and a failed read
103 < 1       writes no `past.json` and never affects the run.
104 < 1     - `provenance.json` — the whole account of the run's changed lines: `hunks`, the changed
105 < 1       lines by task (`path`, `lines`, `task`), and the exceptions only (`contested`, `lost`,
106 < 1       `ordered`, `foreign`). (The coverage pass that added `unproven` was retired unread, #1442.)
107 < 1       The PR body's
108 < 1       `### Provenance` line counts it: a hunk whose `task` joins several with `|` (lines two tasks
109 < 1       wrote identically) counts each of its tasks once.
110 < 1     - `checks-digest.json` — one row per file of the engine's `checks/` folder, in file-name
111 < 1       order: `file`, `copy` (the name's text after its last `@`), `mtime`, and the result's
112 < 1       `clause`, `exit`, `stage` and `diff` (whether it carried one); a file that is not JSON
113 < 1       carries `unreadable: true` instead (#1491). No diff or store is copied.
114 < 1     Not kept: the whole snapshots, the raw weave log (whose `content` is the files' full texts) and
115 < 1     `checks/` itself. (History: the retired factory engine wrote none of these eight.)
116 < 1   - `ultra/integration-run-<N>` on the target — the work, and the only branch the product repository
117 < 1     ever receives. Pushed only when it is ahead of `base=`; the PR's head.
118 < 1     It has three fates, decided by the pull request with the highest `number` on that head:
119 < 1     a merged one goes with the merge (delete-on-merge), a `hold=1` run's stays while its PR is open,
120 < 1     and the retire sweep deletes one whose pull request is closed and not merged.
121 < 1 - **`$RUN_DIR/base`** — one line, a 40-hex sha, written by the engine only when a run that follows
122 < 1   main (policy `flock.main.mode = peer`) moved its base: the main the run's commit sits on. The boot
123 < 1   reads it after the engine exits and judges "nothing ahead of base" and the publish's catch-up
124 < 1   against it; absent, the base is the assignment's `base=`.
125 < 1 - **`refs/ultrapowers/weave/v1` on the target** — main's memory (`factory/flock/weave_store.py`), the
126 < 1   one ref outside `refs/heads/` the product repository receives, and only under policy
127 < 1   `flock.main.mode = memory`. One commit per tree of the default branch, subject `weave <tree sha>`,
128 < 1   holding the Manyana weave of every path changed since the store began. It is not a branch: a
129 < 1   clone does not fetch it, branch protection does not cover it, and nothing reads it but a sandbox.
130 < 1   The publish pushes it after the pull request is open and after each catch-up, always before a
131 < 1   merge request and never after one, with a lease on the tip it was built on; a push that fails
132 < 1   costs only that landing's memory. Appended to, never rewritten: a changed recipe is `v2`.
133 < 1   Deleting the ref forgets the memory and nothing else.
134 < 1 - **The one tag** — a run's record, and the only ref that outlives it. At the end of every run
135 < 1   (done, parked and failed) the sandbox cuts one tag `<owner>-<repo>/run-<N>` in the evidence
136 < 1   repository on the live branch's last commit, verifies it against the remote with
137 < 1   `git ls-remote --tags`, and only then deletes `live/<owner>-<repo>/run-<N>`; a tag that does not
138 < 1   verify keeps the branch. The plan commit is an ancestor of that tag, so one tag carries both the
139 < 1   plan and the record. The record is read by tag:
140 < 1   `runs/<owner>-<repo>/<N>/status.json?ref=<owner>-<repo>/run-<N>` and
141 < 1   `runs/<owner>-<repo>/<N>/plan.md?ref=<owner>-<repo>/run-<N>`, and at
142 < 1   `?ref=live/<owner>-<repo>/run-<N>` while the run is live. No engine reads `gate-verdicts.json`; the
143 < 1   laptop's authoring census (`skills/ultrawrite/scripts/authoring_census.py --fetch`) reads it off the
144 < 1   run tag (runs 269–271 were launched without it, 2026-09-29). The evidence repository's `main` holds
145 < 1   a one-time hand archive under `archive/`; no tool writes to `main`, and runs live only under
146 < 1   `runs/`, on `live/*` branches and on the run tags. The janitor deletes an
147 < 1   `ultra/integration-run-<N>` whose pull request is closed and not merged (`--target
148 < 1   <owner>/<repo>` for a target no fleet VM names).
149 < 1 - **Migration (retired, #1450):** every past target's `ultra/*` runs were copied into the
150 < 1   evidence repository (12 targets, 369 runs, 0 missing, read 2026-09-30); the one-time tool and the
151 < 1   launcher's unmigrated-target refusal are gone. The old refs stay on the targets, untouched.
152 < 1 - **Comment** (≤200 bytes, one line, space-separated `key=value`, this order, nothing else):
153 < 1   `run=<N> plan=<40-hex> target=<owner>/<repo> base=<40-hex> engine=<40-hex>` then
154 < 1   optional `kind=flock` then optional `hold=1`. The boot always runs the Flock
155 < 1   (`factory/flock/engine.mjs`): `kind=flock` or no `kind=` boots it, and `kind=factory` (the retired
156 < 1   engine) fails the run at the assignment; `fleet/launch.mjs` always writes `kind=flock` and refuses `--kind`.
157 < 1   `plan=` is the plan commit — the tip of `live/<owner>-<repo>/run-<N>` in the evidence repository
158 < 1   when the launcher pushed it (the comment carries no `evidence=`; the VM learns the repository from
159 < 1   the setup script); `hold=1` keeps the pull request open for a
160 < 1   person — the sandbox publishes it and does not merge it. Written once by `new --comment`; the sandbox
161 < 1   reads it ONCE from `https://reflection.int.exe.xyz/comment` (`{"comment": "..."}`) and fails the run
162 < 1   if it is absent or malformed. Nobody rewrites it.
163 < 1 - **Proof environment:** one variable, set by the engine and by nothing else. A plan's `Check:`
164 < 1   lines run at every fold check with `ULTRA_BASE` set to the run's base sha — never the anchor,
165 < 1   never a candidate's own base — and it rides no other line. A run's proof is the plan's `Run:`
166 < 1   probes and `Check:` lines only: a task is measured by its `Run:` probes, run in the candidate's
167 < 1   own clone, and by the plan's `Check:` lines on the folded tree; the Flock selects no other tests
168 < 1   (probes-only, operator, 2026-09-27) — no file is written for it anywhere on the fleet. A task's `Run:` probes run with the
169 < 1   engine's own environment and nothing added: no `ULTRA_BASE` (that rides `Check:` lines and
170 < 1   nothing else), no `ULTRA_TASK`, no `ULTRA_RUN_DIR`, no `ULTRA_EXAM_PASS`. Those three, the
171 < 1   numbered exam passes, the pre-review pass, the review rounds and `reviewOnStateExams` (#836) were
172 < 1   the wave engine's and left at cut two (2026-09-21); a probe that wants a base to compare against
173 < 1   carries a frozen literal (ultrawrite §Proof), and a comparison against BASE is a `Check:`. The
174 < 1   peer exam role, its files and their
175 < 1   evidence copy left the engine at cut three (2026-09-22); `--engine d412149a` runs the engine from
176 < 1   before it.
177 < 1 - **State handshake:** left with the factory at cut three (2026-09-22); nothing reads it. Its
178 < 1   shape is #812 (4a63b4ef); map #998 dropped it on 2026-09-30, and #1419 (the Flock's beliefs) is
179 < 1   where a reached state would return.
180 < 1 - **Launch order (launcher):** read the evidence repository (`--evidence-repo`, else `fleet.json`'s
181 < 1   `"evidence"`) and refuse a launch with neither, naming the key and `node fleet/doctor.mjs` →
182 < 1   validate `--target`/`--base`/plan — a `--base` that is not an ancestor
183 < 1   of the target's default branch is refused (the publish fold would have nothing to fold onto), and so
184 < 1   is a shallow launch clone, whose history cannot answer that question → the `--base` check
185 < 1   and the parse both run files fetched at `engine=` — `skills/ultrapowers/scripts/plan_check.py` and
186 < 1   the `plan_parse.py` it imports, the sandbox's own parser (beside its other fact lines,
187 < 1   `plan_check.py` prints one `SHARED fact:` line per path two or more tasks list — a fact, never a
188 < 1   refusal),
189 < 1   at that sha, `git show` from the laptop's plugin checkout, else `gh api` from popmechanic/ultrapowers,
190 < 1   into a temp directory — and a launch whose two files cannot be fetched is refused before any push;
191 < 1   the launch line carries `compiler=<sha>` → read every command word of every task's `Run:`
192 < 1   probes and of the plan's `Check:` lines against `SANDBOX_TOOLCHAIN` in `fleet/toolchain.mjs` (the
193 < 1   image's tools plus the delta `fleet/setup-script.mjs` installs: node, bun, celld, pytest) and
194 < 1   refuse a word outside it, one line per word — `launch: task <id>: probe runner '<word>' is not
195 < 1   in the sandbox toolchain — <line>`, `check` where a task id would be — before anything is
196 < 1   pushed (#645) → read the pool
197 < 1   (`ssh exe.dev "billing plan --json"`) and refuse a run larger than it → run the janitor
198 < 1   (`fleet/janitor.mjs`, the reap) → refuse a plan that is already live on the target (#1036): the
200 > 1   plan text's git blob sha is compared with
199 < 2   plan text's git blob sha — the `<plan sha>` of the kata `Idempotency-Key` below — is compared with
200 < 1   the `runs/<owner>-<repo>/<N>/plan.md` blob on `live/<owner>-<repo>/run-<N>` in the evidence
201 < 1   repository, for every running `fleet-r*` VM whose comment names this target and whose record
203 > 1   does not say the run ended; a
202 < 2   (hub, else evidence) does not say the run ended; a
203 < 1   match is a `Refusal` naming `run-<N>`, the VM and `--again`, the one flag that launches it again on
204 < 1   purpose, and a run whose record says it ended never refuses, however recently → `git ls-remote` the
205 < 1   evidence repository's `live/<owner>-<repo>/run-*` branches and `<owner>-<repo>/run-*` tags for N
206 < 1   → refuse when `integrations list --json`
207 < 1   has no `gh-<owner>-<repo>` for the target or none for the evidence repository (the fix named is
208 < 1   `node fleet/target.mjs <owner>/<repo>` for whichever is missing; a public target would still clone
209 < 1   from github.com but could not push or open its PR, so it is not launched) →
210 < 1   `node fleet/claude-token.mjs refresh` →
211 < 1   read the account's usage windows (`node fleet/claude-token.mjs usage --json --account <account>
212 < 1   --no-rotate`) and refuse a reading at or past 95% of either window, naming the account and the
213 < 1   reset time, before any push (#1114); under the wall, the launch line carries
214 < 1   `usage: <account> 7d <n>% resets <iso>; 5h <n>% resets <iso>` →
216 > 2   kata, only under the rollback. The `launch.kata.mode` cell of `fleet/policy.json` is `off` (the
217 < 2   operator's decision of 2026-11-13: Kata leaves the run path): the launcher does not open
218 < 2   `~/.ultrapowers/kata-hub.env`, pings no hub, files nothing, and the plan commit carries no
219 < 2   `kata.json` — and since the sandbox writes to Kata only when that file is there (the Flock's
220 < 2   `kata:mirror`, the boot's `board.mjs close-run` and `mark-run`), a run writes nothing to the hub,
221 < 2   on any engine version; its `events.jsonl` holds one `board:close` or `board:mark` row saying
222 < 2   `skipped: no kata.json to read`, and a hub that is down refuses no launch. A run is read from its
223 < 2   evidence record (`fleet/run-read.mjs`, `--follow` to watch). Under the rollback, `mode` `on`,
224 < 2   everything from here to the push is as before: the run filed on the hub, for each push attempt's N and before that attempt's plan commit is
215 < 2   kata: the run filed on the hub, for each push attempt's N and before that attempt's plan commit is
216 < 2   built — `plan_parse.py <plan>` (the launch's second call; the old compiler's per-task `factsheet`
217 < 2   left with it at cut B, 2026-09-21 — nothing in the factory read it), one project `<owner>-<repo>` (slashes in the
218 < 2   target become `-`) — one project per target and not one per run, so a name the hub already holds
219 < 2   answers the existing project and this run files into it; every kata behaviour this paragraph
220 < 2   leans on is one reading of `node fleet/tests/probe_kata_facts.mjs` and one row of this
221 < 2   contract's `Kata facts (measured)` list — one run issue (`run-N: <plan H1>`, body
222 < 2   the plan's `**Claim:**` line, metadata `{run, target, base, closes, plan, "work.branch"}` — `closes` the numbers of the
223 < 2   `**Closes:**` line, `[]` when absent; `plan` the plan text's blob sha; `work.branch`
224 < 2   `ultra/integration-run-<N>` (#1391) — created with `force_new: true`, because the hub scores a
225 < 2   title against the project's open issues and a replayed plan's run issue differs from the earlier
226 < 2   run's only by N; the task creates carry no such field), one issue per task in wave order created under an
227 < 2   `Idempotency-Key` `<target>:<plan sha>:task-<id>` (`<plan sha>` the plan text's git blob sha) whose
228 < 2   create body is the same on every launch of that plan text — `task <id>: <title>`, empty body,
229 < 2   metadata `{task, plan}`, no links, since kata fingerprints the key with those fields — and then
230 < 2   read back, its metadata patched `{run, wave}` under that read's revision and the run
231 < 2   issue set as its `parent` with `replace: true`; one `blocks` link per `dag_edges` entry created ON
232 < 2   the task that blocks, then one `getIssue` per task and one for the run, whose revisions are what
233 < 2   `kata.json` records; a refused push that bumps N closes the run-N issue with reason
234 < 2   `wontfix` and files again for N+1, where the same keys answer the same task issues — nothing on the
235 < 2   hub is destroyed. The hub is reached from the laptop as `ssh <hub> curl …
236 < 2   localhost:8000/api/v1/…` — the host is the `KATA_URL` of `~/.ultrapowers/kata-hub.env`, the bearer
237 < 2   is sourced from `/etc/kata/kata.env` ON the hub and never rides a laptop argv; an absent env file
238 < 2   is refused before any command (`node fleet/kata-hub.mjs` builds the hub), a `ping` that fails —
239 < 2   asked right after the `integrations list --json` read — is refused before any push, and a hub call
240 < 2   that fails after it is a launch failure before any push and before `new` →
241 < 1   push the parentless plan commit to `live/<owner>-<repo>/run-<N>` in the evidence repository → ONE
242 < 1   verb:
243 < 1 
244 < 1   ```
245 < 1   ssh exe.dev "new --name fleet-r<N>-<yymmddHHMM>-<4 hex> --tag fleet --comment '<assignment>' \
246 < 1     --cpu <cpu> --memory <memory> --setup-script /dev/stdin --json"
247 < 1   ```
248 < 1 
249 < 1   with the generated setup script on the verb's stdin, under a `# fleet: width=<W>`
250 < 1   header line the
251 < 1   launcher stamps on it — W is not an assignment key (`COMMENT_KEYS` spells six and
252 < 1   `parse_assignment` fails the boot on a seventh), so that header is a record only: the old wave
253 < 1   engine read the same W back off its own compile (`args.json`) as its dispatch bound, falling back
254 < 1   to 12 when the compile answered no waves, and that reading left with it at cut two (2026-09-21).
255 < 1   `<cpu>` and `<memory>` are the PLAN's size,
256 < 1   not the fleet's: the launcher parses the plan once before this verb (`plan_parse.py <plan>`,
258 > 1   the one payload the sizing reads), takes
257 < 2   the one payload the sizing and the kata filing both read), takes
258 < 1   W — the task count of the widest `launch_waves` entry —
259 < 1   and asks for `min(cpu, 2 + ceil(W / 3))` vCPU and
260 < 1   `min(memory, 2 + W)GB`, where the `cpu`/`memory` pair of `~/.ultrapowers/fleet.json` (or
261 < 1   `FLEET_DEFAULTS`) is the CEILING; `--cpu` or `--memory` on the launch line wins outright, and
262 < 1   either number is refused when `billing plan --json` cannot seat it. The browser term the old
263 < 1   state exams added to memory left with them (cut three, 2026-09-22; a return is owed on #1248).
264 < 1   The verb carries NO `--integration`, and the launcher refuses its own line
265 < 1   before issuing it should the flag ever reappear. The run's credentials reach the VM by TAG instead:
266 < 1   each integration a run needs — `claude-max`, the target's `gh-<owner>-<repo>` and the evidence
267 < 1   repository's `gh-<owner>-<repo>` — is attached to `tag:fleet` (at creation with `--attach tag:fleet`,
268 < 1   or later with `integrations attach <name> tag:fleet`), so `--tag fleet` on `new` is the grant and
269 < 1   nothing is attached per VM at all. (exe.dev tried an attachment-policy model on 2026-09-11 and
270 < 1   rolled it back the same afternoon; `integrations` has no `policy` verb, #1434.) exe.dev documents no
271 < 1   order between the grant taking effect and the setup script starting, so the script waits — bounded,
272 < 1   thirty reads two seconds apart — until Reflection's `/integrations` lists `claude-max` before it
273 < 1   starts the unit. And `cp` of a fleet VM copies its tags by default, so a copy inherits every
274 < 1   credential and the janitor's reap — a forensic copy is taken with tag copying off.
275 < 1   There is no separate `attach`, no ssh-readiness wait and no explicit start: the setup script starts
276 < 1   the unit as its last act.
277 < 1 - **Setup script (generated by `fleet/setup-script.mjs`, ≤10 KiB, bash, `set -euo pipefail`):** exe.dev
278 < 1   runs it ONCE, as `exedev`, on first boot. It contains no secret and no `ANTHROPIC` string. Its duties,
279 < 1   in order:
280 < 1   1. wait, bounded, for the user bus (`systemctl --user` has no address before `user@.service` is
281 < 1      up);
283 > 1   2. install the toolchain: node 24.22.1, bun 1.4.4,
282 < 2   2. install the toolchain: node 24.22.1, bun 1.4.4 — and no kata: a sandbox runs none, the engine
283 < 2      writing the hub directly (#1390) —
284 < 1      celld 0.6.1 (the one `.gz` asset from
285 < 1      `github.com/denoland/celld`, verified with `sha256sum -c` against the digest the plugin records
286 < 1      beside bun's version — `CELLD_SHA256` in `fleet/setup-script.mjs`, since the release carries no
287 < 1      sums file and `gh attestation verify` needs a token the sandbox does not hold — then decompressed
288 < 1      and installed at `/usr/local/bin/celld` mode 0755, never through the vendor's installer script
289 < 1      and never under `/usr/local/lib/fleet`), and pytest by the image's `uv pip install --system --break-system-packages`, pinned to
290 < 1      `pytest==7.4.4` and `pytest-xdist==3.4.0` with their dependencies `pluggy==1.4.0`,
291 < 1      `packaging==24.0`, `iniconfig==1.1.1` and `execnet==2.0.0` — exactly the versions noble's
292 < 1      packages shipped; uv is a package manager and queries PyPI, but with all six pinned it
293 < 1      resolves nothing fresh. The node, bun and celld fetch-and-verify jobs run at once,
294 < 1      each its own background job waited on by its own pid, so any failure still stops the script;
295 < 1      each step is preceded by a `stamp` line (UTC to the millisecond, `setup: <step>`) in
296 < 1      `$HOME/fleet-setup.log`, and a last `stamp done` follows the unit start;
297 < 1   3. install the bootstrap at `/usr/local/lib/fleet/bootstrap.sh`, mode 0555, owned by root — outside
298 < 1      `/home/exedev` and unwritable by the run;
299 < 1   4. install the user unit TEMPLATE `~/.config/systemd/user/fleet-run@.service`
300 < 1      (`Description=ultrapowers run %i`, `After=network-online.target`, `Type=exec`,
301 < 1      `RemainAfterExit=yes`, `RuntimeMaxSec=6h`, `LimitNOFILE=524288` — a shell under the unit
302 < 1      otherwise inherits soft 1024, which is what bites Chromium, bun and pytest, while the image's
303 < 1      hard limit for a service is 524288 — `ExecStart=/usr/local/lib/fleet/bootstrap.sh %i`,
304 < 1      `ExecStopPost=/usr/local/lib/fleet/bootstrap.sh %i died`, no `[Install]`, no `KillMode`, no
305 < 1      `Restart`). The stop-post is how the VM writes its own death (#1445): systemd runs it when the
306 < 1      unit stops — after a non-zero exit, a kill or the `RuntimeMaxSec` timeout — with
307 < 1      `SERVICE_RESULT`, `EXIT_CODE` and `EXIT_STATUS` set, and it reaches `boot.sh died` through the
308 < 1      bootstrap; after a clean exit the unit stays `active (exited)` and the stop-post runs only when
309 < 1      the VM goes down, where `died` finds a finished record and writes nothing;
310 < 1   5. write `~/.claude/settings.json`, exactly
311 < 1      `{"env":{"CLAUDE_CODE_PRINT_BG_WAIT_CEILING_MS":"0"},"permissions":{"defaultMode":"bypassPermissions"}}`,
312 < 1      and a git identity for `exedev` (`user.name fleet`, `user.email fleet@exe.dev`). No `ANTHROPIC_*`
313 < 1      anywhere in the script: the proxy variables are the engine service's argv (boot script, below),
314 < 1      and the bearer itself never leaves the edge;
315 < 1   6. write the evidence repository's `<owner>/<repo>` to `$HOME/fleet-evidence-repo` — the one way
316 < 1      the VM learns it (the assignment comment carries no `evidence=`);
317 < 1   7. wait for the user bus (`${FLEET_USER_BUS:-/run/user/$(id -u)/bus}`, at most 60 s) to appear
318 < 1      before any `systemctl --user` call — the image lingers `exedev` by a marker file, so the script
319 < 1      never calls `loginctl`;
320 < 1   8. `systemctl --user daemon-reload`, then `systemctl --user start fleet-run@<N>.service`.
321 < 1 
322 < 1   `<N>` is baked into the script the launcher generates, so the script passes `bash -n` for every run
323 < 1   number. Why a template of `Type=exec` and not a oneshot (Counsel 3, measured on exeuntu, systemd 255):
324 < 1   a oneshot has `TimeoutStartUSec=infinity`, ignores `RuntimeMaxSec=`, and finished reads
325 < 1   `inactive/dead` — indistinguishable from never started.
326 < 1   `systemctl --user show fleet-run@<N>.service -p ActiveState -p SubState -p Result -p ExecMainStatus`
327 < 1   reads: `active/exited` + `success` = done; `failed` + `ExecMainStatus=N` = crashed with exit N;
328 < 1   `failed` + `Result=timeout` = over the 6 h budget; `inactive/dead` = never launched.
329 < 1 - **Bootstrap (`fleet/fleet-bootstrap.sh`, installed as `/usr/local/lib/fleet/bootstrap.sh`, ≤40 lines,
330 < 1   bash, `set -euo pipefail`):** read the comment once → when `$1` (the unit's `%i`) is given, it and the
331 < 1   comment's `run=` agree or the run fails → parse `engine=` (40 hex or fail) →
332 < 1   `dst=/home/exedev/engines/<sha>`; if absent, clone `https://github.com/popmechanic/ultrapowers.git`
333 < 1   to `$dst.tmp`, `git checkout -q <sha>`, `mv` → `exec "$dst/factory/boot.sh" boot` with
334 < 1   `FLEET_ASSIGNMENT='<comment>'` in its env. It never writes anywhere but `/home/exedev/engines/` and
335 < 1   `/home/exedev/fleet-boot.log`. It is never overwritten by a run. The assignment comes from
336 < 1   Reflection, never from `$1`. An optional `$2` is the mode: absent is `boot`, as above; `died` is
337 < 1   the unit's `ExecStopPost=`, the VM writing its own death (#1445) — the comment is read as in
338 < 1   `boot`, an absent `engines/<sha>` logs `died: no engine at <sha> — nothing to write` and exits 0
339 < 1   (never a clone at stop time), and otherwise it `exec`s `"$dst/factory/boot.sh" died` with
340 < 1   `FLEET_ASSIGNMENT` set exactly as `boot` does. In `died` mode every refusal that `boot` exits 1 on
341 < 1   exits 0 after its log line.
342 < 1 - **Boot script (`factory/boot.sh`), invoked by the bootstrap:** reads the evidence repository from
343 < 1   `$HOME/fleet-evidence-repo` and fails the run without it, clones the target at `base=`, fetches
344 < 1   only `live/<owner>-<repo>/run-<N>` of the evidence repository, shallow, through the same edge host
345 < 1   (`https://$GITHUB_INT_HOST/<evidence repo>.git`), parses the plan once with `plan_parse.py`
346 < 1   into `$FLEET_HOME/plans/<run>.plan.json` (a refusal fails the run; the engine, the catch-up, the
347 < 1   PR body and the publish probe all read that parse, #1449), extracts the previous run's folder for the
348 < 1   engine's `--past-dir`, updates Claude Code to the newest release (`timeout 120 sudo -n exeuntu update claude`,
349 < 1   unpinned) and fails the run below the floor `2.1.287` — the first release with mods — logging
350 < 1   the release it runs (`claude: <version>` in `fleet-boot.log`) and setting `DISABLE_AUTOUPDATER=1`
351 < 1   for the run, runs the engine as one transient unit, commits the record to the live
353 > 1   branch every 10 s and at each transition (see above), cuts and verifies the run tag and deletes
352 < 2   branch every 60 s and at each transition (see above), cuts and verifies the run tag and deletes
353 < 1   the live branch at the end of every run, and — only when there is something to publish — opens the pull request
354 < 1   and, gated by `factory/policy.json`'s `publish.self_merge`, merges it — the pull request, the
355 < 1   merge, the catch-up and the publish probe are `factory/publish.mjs`, which the boot calls once
356 < 1   after the engine unit exits (#1441), GitHub over `curl` and the target over `git`, both off PATH. When the target's tip moved
357 < 1   underneath the run, the boot catches the run up to the new main inline by running
358 < 1   `node factory/flock/catchup.mjs --plan <plan> --plan-json <parse> --target <target> --base <run base> --onto <moved tip>
359 < 1   --run-dir <dir>`, whose last stdout line is JSON: `{"refolded": true, "head", "onto"}` (exit 0) or
360 < 1   `{"refolded": false, "reason": "conflict" | "red", "onto"}` (exit 1). It joins the run's work onto
361 < 1   the new main through the weave keeper (`factory/flock/weave.py`) and re-runs the plan's setup,
362 < 1   every task's probes and the run-wide check with `ULTRA_BASE` set to the new main; on exit 0 the
363 < 1   boot force-pushes the new head over the run's branch, and on a conflict or a red check it leaves
364 < 1   the run's own commit alone, so the boot opens a draft. The catch-up runs under the same
365 < 1   environment as the engine unit — the same `env -u CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR …` prefix, so anything it
366 < 1   asks reaches the proxy and the edge-injected bearer (run-207, 2026-09-21, n=1 run). The engine
367 < 1   unit itself:
368 < 1   - engine: `systemd-run --user --unit=fleet-engine-<N> --pipe --wait --collect -p MemoryMax=40G -p MemorySwapMax=0 -p LimitNOFILE=524288 -p RuntimeMaxSec=<seconds> -p WorkingDirectory=<target>
369 < 1     -- env -u CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=<proxy> CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=placeholder
370 < 1     TYPESAFE_BASE_URL=https://typesafe.int.exe.xyz ULTRAPOWERS_FLEET_RUN=<run id> node
371 < 1     <engine>/factory/flock/engine.mjs --plan <plan> --plan-json <parse> --target <target> --base <sha> --run-dir <dir>`,
372 < 1     stdout+stderr teed to `engine.log`.
373 < 1     cwd `<target>` — the same working directory the unit's own `WorkingDirectory=` sets.
374 < 1   **This bullet spelled out the wave engine's own boot script here in detail through 2026-09-21** —
375 < 1   the kata ping and reuse-driven relaunch, the five declared worker roles and their confinement,
376 < 1   the worker API-layer error classes, the fold-before-publish transient unit and its six
377 < 1   dispositions, the fold-again wall-clock and the tag-and-branch record step — and all of it left
378 < 1   with that engine at cut two; what `factory/boot.sh` itself does at this level of detail is not
379 < 1   yet written down here.
380 < 2 - **Kata record (engine):** When a run carries a kata record, a worker's env names the hub it talks
381 < 2   to as `KATA_SERVER` (the record's own `url`, or the fallback `https://kata.int.exe.xyz` when it
382 < 2   names none), the placeholder bearer the edge replaces on the way out as
383 < 2   `KATA_AUTH_TOKEN=edge-injects-the-bearer` (no real token is ever written to disk or argv), the
384 < 2   label the run spells its events with as `KATA_AUTHOR=<label>@<run id>`, and — for a label whose
385 < 2   task the record knows — that task's issue as `KATA_REF=<project>#<short id>`; a label naming no
386 < 2   task (`integration`, a reconcile worker) carries the first three and no `KATA_REF`. The same
387 < 2   worker's issue is stamped by two hooks on its session: `kata attention-hook start` and
388 < 2   `kata attention-hook end`, exiting 0 doing nothing when the worker carries no `KATA_REF`.
389 < 2 - **Kata record (Flock, the `kata:mirror` row):** the Flock mirrors only when the boot passes
390 < 2   `--kata-url`, `--kata-json` and `--kata-actor` (the boot does exactly when
391 < 2   `$FLEET_HOME/plans/<run>.kata.json` exists); it posts to the hub at `https://kata.int.exe.xyz`,
392 < 2   reading the project id from that record. Each board move becomes one comment on its task's issue, posted after the move has
393 < 2   completed: `claimed by <agent>`, `<agent> released: <why>` (the engine's own release reason,
394 < 2   e.g. `A released: gave up`), `parked: <why>` (a task parked at its release cap, e.g.
395 < 2   `parked: A released: gave up`), `reopened: <why>` and `done by <agent>` (a
396 < 2   release made on the way to a reopen posts only the `reopened:` body). Every attempted post is
397 < 2   one `kata:mirror` event row in `events.jsonl` naming the task, the body, `key` (the
398 < 2   `Idempotency-Key` sent), `ok` (true only when the hub answered with a comment uid),
399 < 2   `comment_uid` and `created_at`; the key is `<run uid>:task:<id>:<n>` (`n` counting that task's
400 < 2   posts from 1) or `<run uid>:run:<n>` on the run issue, `<run uid>` being the record's `run.uid`. A
401 < 2   task the record names no issue for posts nothing and its row says it was skipped. Posts are
402 < 2   fire-and-forget: none is awaited, so a slow, failing or absent Kata never delays or fails the
403 < 2   run — a failed post is that row and nothing else. One task's posts go out one after another,
404 < 2   in board order; each gives up after 10 seconds, and at exit the engine gives posts still queued
405 < 2   or in flight at most 3 seconds, then leaves without them. Before this (radio-station run-1,
406 < 2   2026-09-28, n=1 run) the Flock posted nothing and the board showed only the launcher's filing
407 < 2   and the boot's closes.
408 < 1 - **Beliefs (Flock, the `belief` row):** a builder's belief is the tuple
409 < 1   `{by, claim, about, task, path, stale}` — `about` is one of `task`, `app` or `engine` (whom the
410 < 1   belief is for: the task's builders, the app's operator, or the engine's maintainers), the claim
411 < 1   is at most 300 characters, and `path` (a repo-relative file) and `stale` (exact text still wrong
412 < 1   there) are optional. A belief carries no confidence: the author's own number (0.85–0.95 over
413 < 1   run-277..291, never revised) ranked a wrong belief over its correction. Every belief posted is
415 > 1   kept as one `belief` row in `events.jsonl`, whatever its `about`, and `fleet/run-read.mjs` prints
416 < 1   each builder's as a `<builder> noted: <claim>` line, in `--follow` while the run is still going.
417 < 1   (Until 2026-11-13 an `engine` belief was also posted to the kata hub as a comment.) A belief naming a `path` is a loose end: with
414 < 2   kept as one `belief` row in `events.jsonl`, whatever its `about`. Every `engine` belief is also
415 < 2   posted to Kata, while the run is still going, as the comment
416 < 2   `engine belief from <builder>: <claim>` on its task's issue, or on the run issue when the belief
417 < 2   names no task the record knows; it goes out as the mirror's posts do (fire-and-forget, a
418 < 2   `kata:mirror` row) and shows in `--follow`. A belief naming a `path` is a loose end: with
419 < 1   `flock.loose_ends.mode` `work` (policy; rollback `record`) the engine adds one `L:loose` task
420 < 1   before settling when a loose end is open or not checked. One `loose-ends` row per run marks each
421 < 1   loose end open, resolved or unchecked against the final snapshot, and the PR body lists the open
422 < 1   and unchecked ones under `### Loose ends`.
423 < 1   A draft after `L:loose` is added lands the pre-cleanup green snapshot as ready, recorded as a
424 < 1   `loose:fallback` row and a second `settled` row, and the PR's Loose ends section says the cleanup
425 < 1   was dropped; a losing peer-rewrite read on that pre-cleanup snapshot still ends draft.
426 < 1 - **Scope (Flock, the `scope:outside` and `driver:amendment` rows, #1333):** the Flock's edge
427 < 1   refuses to settle on a snapshot that changes a path outside every task's Files when no builder
428 < 1   wrote that change; each such path is one `scope:outside` row in `events.jsonl` and the snapshot
429 < 1   does not settle. A builder's own change to a path outside every task's Files is let through and
430 < 1   recorded as a `driver:amendment` row naming the builder and the path. The switch is
431 < 1   `flock.scope` in `factory/policy.json` (`mode = enforce`); `flock.scope.mode = record` is the
432 < 1   rollback — the rows are still written and nothing is refused. Before this (run-247, #1322) a
433 < 1   green run merged a corrupted file no task listed.
434 < 1 - **Stall (Flock, the `stall:no-progress` row, #1334):** a Flock run whose best-green count has not
435 < 1   risen for `flock.stall.minutes` (20), with at least one builder session ended since it last rose
436 < 1   (one long session is never cut off), and whose queued snapshots are all tested, writes one
437 < 1   `stall:no-progress` row in `events.jsonl` and ends as a draft, alerting, instead of running to its
438 < 1   clock. The window opens when the builders start, not before setup. The stop interrupts every live
439 < 1   session, starts no new one, and counts none of them as a give-back. Every stall row is named
440 < 1   `stall:<kind>`. `--stall-minutes <n>` is an engine flag for a hand run (the launcher and boot do
441 < 1   not pass it; a fleet run reads the cell), and a value that is not a number of minutes, 0 or more,
442 < 1   refuses with exit 2. The cell is
443 < 1   `flock.stall` in `factory/policy.json`; `flock.stall.minutes = 0` is the rollback — no limit, the
444 < 1   run ends only at its clock. Before this (runs 247, 251–255 and radio-station run-1, 2026-09-26..28,
445 < 1   n=7) the longest wait for a rise in the green count was 2.0 min, while run-252 ran 229.7 min
446 < 1   after its last rise.
447 < 1 - **Jev (2026-09-16, the `jev:` seam):** one event kind rides the run's own event log exactly as
448 < 1   a `driver:` row does — `jev:step` (the step read for a plan clause, one row per clause asked;
449 < 1   `factory/record.mjs` reads the latest row per clause) — each one a `POST /v1/systemone` against
450 < 1   the typesafe host, asked only over a reachable TypeSafe edge; a failed call is one log line and
451 < 1   no row.
452 < 1     Receipts (2026-09-16): the same POST carries a `state` object out and an `answers` object back;
453 < 1   neither is persisted anywhere this engine reads again.
454 < 1 - **status.json:** `{"run":"<N>","state":"booting|running|publishing|done|parked|failed","phase":"<text>","pr":"<url or null>","prAuthor":"<GitHub login or null>","merged":"<40-hex or null>","branch":"ultra/integration-run-<N>","vm":"<vm_name>","startedAt":"<iso>","updatedAt":"<iso>","error":"<string or null>","tasks":{"<id>":{"state":"folded|failed","park":"<detail or null>"}}}`
455 < 1   — committed to
456 < 1   `runs/<owner>-<repo>/<N>/status.json` on `live/<owner>-<repo>/run-<N>` in the evidence repository
457 < 1   at every transition **and, while
458 < 1   the engine runs, on the first tick that finds `events.jsonl` changed since the last commit, at
460 > 1   most once every `FLEET_COMMIT_SECONDS` seconds (default 10)**. A tick that saw no change is a
459 < 2   most once every `FLEET_COMMIT_SECONDS` seconds (default 60)**. A tick that saw no change is a
460 < 1   heartbeat (`updatedAt` moves) and earns no commit.
461 < 1   `"tasks":` is the LAST cell on the page — a reader answers the FIRST `"state"` in the file, so a
462 < 1   task's own `folded` must never sit above the run's — and it is a projection of `events.jsonl` and
463 < 1   nothing else: one key per task a `landing` or `parked` row names, in the order it first appears,
464 < 1   each carrying its `state` — `folded` for a `landing` row, `failed` for a `parked` row, a later row
465 < 1   for the same task overwriting — and its `park`, the `reason` its `parked` row carried, else `null`.
466 < 1   The same projection runs inside `factory/record.mjs` (`status`), called by `write_status` each
467 < 1   time it writes the page, rather than as a separate invocation over a log file.
468 < 1   `phase` names the SUB-STEP while the engine runs: the run's last phase event alone when no worker
469 < 1   is open, and `<phase> · <sub>` — that phase, a space, `·`, a space, and either the label of the
470 < 1   most recent worker still running or the kind of the last event — otherwise. That last event is the
471 < 1   run's OWN PROGRESS and not the bookkeeping written to the same log: the projection SKIPS the kinds
473 > 1   that are not progress — `transcript:*`, `engine:log` and `capture:*`, and `resolver:reply`
472 < 2   that are not progress — `kata:*`, `transcript:*`, `engine:log` and `capture:*`, and `resolver:reply`
473 < 1   with them — and the kind it takes is the last `driver:*`/`worker:*` one the log has written since
474 < 1   that phase event, so a phase followed only by bookkeeping reads as that phase alone.
475 < 1   The `state` cell is a sequence, not a set: a run that published reads
476 < 1   `booting → running → publishing → done`, and a run whose merge PUT answered a base-moved 405 folds
477 < 1   again — ONE `running → publishing` PAIR PER FOLD, the `running` carrying that fold's own phase
478 < 1   `publish fold (attempt <n>)`. So a run folded once more reads
479 < 1   `running → publishing → running → publishing → done`, one folded three times more carries three
480 < 1   such pairs before its `done`, and the count is whatever the folds allowed under the refold bound
481 < 1   `publish.self_merge.max_refolds` in `factory/policy.json` — never a fixed number. `parked` and `failed` are terminal wherever they are reached.
482 < 1 - **Publish:** the pull request is one `POST /repos/<target>/pulls` with title
483 < 1   `fleet run-<N>: <plan H1>`, head `ultra/integration-run-<N>`, base the target's default branch,
484 < 1   `draft` true unless the engine exited 0, and no `authorization` header — the edge injects the
485 < 1   credential. Its body is the plan's `**Summary:**` paragraph, a blank line, the receipt — a
486 < 1   `### Receipt` table with one `| task | probe | proves | exit |` row per `Run:` line of the plan,
487 < 1   the exit read from the `edge` row of the last `settled` snapshot (the last `edge` row when
488 < 1   nothing settled), then `Run-wide checks: exit <n>` and `**Evidence:** <the run tag's folder>` —
489 < 1   `https://github.com/<evidence repo>/tree/<owner>-<repo>/run-<N>/runs/<owner>-<repo>/<N>` — a blank line, and one `Closes #<n>` line per number on the plan's `**Closes:**`
490 < 1   line, all rendered by `factory/record.mjs pr-body`. A run with no `edge` row carries only the
491 < 1   evidence line. The `publish:pr` row it leaves —
492 < 1   `{ts, kind, url, number, draft}` — is appended by `factory/publish.mjs`, as are its `merge`,
493 < 1   `refold` and `publish:*` rows.
494 < 1 - **Publish probe (#835, in `factory/publish.mjs`, run once the merge answered a sha, before the
495 < 1   boot's `write_status`):** the plan's `**Publish:**`/`**Verify:**`/
496 < 1   `**Rollback:**` header lines are read off the run's one parse (never grepped off the plan text);
497 < 1   a plan naming no `**Publish:**` line, or `factory/policy.json`'s `publish.probe.enabled` false
498 < 1   (a cell with no `enabled` key, or no readable file, reads as false), leaves
499 < 1   the run's `phase` at the plain `"the pull request was merged"` and writes no `publish:*` row and
500 < 1   no `publish.json`. Otherwise:
501 < 1   1. **Deploy** — `bash -lc "<deploy cmd>"`, cwd `<target>`, under `timeout <publish.probe.
502 < 1      timeout_seconds>` (policy default 600; exit 124 on the budget), env carrying `CLOUDFLARE_API_BASE_URL=https://
503 < 1      cloudflare.int.exe.xyz/client/v4` and `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=placeholder`. Its combined
504 < 1      stdout+stderr is grepped for the first `https://[A-Za-z0-9.-]*.workers.dev` url; a
505 < 1      `publish:deploy{cmd, exit, ms, url}` row is written (`url` is `null` when none matched). Exit
506 < 1      non-zero, or no url matched, writes `publish.json` (`url` null, `published` false, `verify`
